title: Should File::SOPS::Comment be a Moo class like every other class here?
updated: 2026-08-21T08:59:09Z
---

Question for the maintainer, not a defect. Every other class in this
distribution is Moo + namespace::clean; File::SOPS::Comment is a hand-written
`bless` with `sub new` and `sub text`, which is how ADR 0041 wrote it when it
lived inside Encrypted.pm as a nine-line leaf. karr #147 moved it to
lib/File/SOPS/Comment.pm unchanged -- a move must not move behaviour, and a Moo
constructor answers differently for the three inputs the class refuses (missing
text, a reference, an empty string), whose messages t/56 reads.

So the divergence from the house rule "one object system per distribution" is
now visible in a file of its own. Two defensible answers:

  - Leave it. The class holds one immutable field, has no roles, no lazy
    build, no coercion, and loads nothing -- which is exactly why the ladder
    can load it without a cycle.
  - Convert it. Conformance over taste, at the price of a behaviour-carrying
    edit: `has text => (is => 'ro', required => 1)` plus a BUILD for the
    ref/empty refusals, and t/56's `like($@, qr/cannot be empty/)` and the two
    bare `ok(!defined(eval {...}))` checks re-read against the new messages.

Whichever way it goes, it is a decision about the class and not about the wire:
detect_type, value_to_bytes and the digest see the same object either way.

Lane: wire.
